^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line 37 heap object reachable from static: FAILURE$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line 38 pointer in static: FAILURE$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line 39 pointers are restricted to known values: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line 50 escaped local of caller: FAILURE$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line 51 local without address: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line 59 heap object reachable from operand: FAILURE$
^\[main\.assertion\.7\] line 60 unreachable heap object: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^warning: ignoring
dereference failure.*: FAILURE$
--
The havoc_reachable statement that the approximation of inline assembly emits
is implemented in symbolic execution using the value set: all objects
reachable from the pointer operands (and, with a "memory" clobber, from the
objects of static lifetime and the address-taken local variables of all
functions on the call stack) are assigned nondeterministic values. The
pointers stored in these objects are restricted to previously stored pointers,
the addresses of the reachable objects, and null.
